# Minimum Time to Burn a Binary Tree

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

## Question

Given a binary tree and the value of a target node, fire starts burning from
that target node. Each minute, fire spreads from a burning node to its
directly connected neighbors — this includes its left child,
right child, and also its parent (since a tree, when burning, behaves
like an undirected graph — fire doesn't care about parent/child direction).

Find the minimum time (in minutes) required to burn the entire tree.

Example:

```
Input Tree:
          1
        /   \
       2     3
      / \     \
     4   5     6
        / \
       7   8

target = 5
```

Expected Output: `3`
(Fire spreads: 5 → {2, 7, 8} → {1, ...} → {3} ... taking 3 minutes to cover
every node)

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

## Explanation with Figure

A binary tree only stores `left` and `right` pointers — there is no way to
go from a child back up to its parent. But fire doesn't respect that
one-way structure; it can spread upward too.

So the trick is:

1. Build a `parent` map using one BFS pass over the tree, so every node
   also knows how to reach its parent.
2. Run a second BFS ("multi-source spread") starting from the target
   node, treating each node as having 3 possible neighbors: `left`,
   `right`, and `parent`.
3. Count the number of levels this BFS takes to visit every node —
   that count is the answer.

This is the exact same pattern as "Rotting Oranges" / "Burning Tree" problems.

### Figure: Tree turned into a graph (dotted lines = new parent links)

```
                (1)
               /   \
              /     \
            (2)------(3)
            / \        \
           /   \        \
         (4)   (5)------(6)
               / \
              /   \
            (7)   (8)

   solid lines  = original tree edges (child -> parent already broken)
   the parent map re-adds the missing "child -> parent" edge
   once added, every node has up to 3 neighbors: left, right, parent
```

### Figure: BFS spread ("burning") starting from node 5

```
Time 0:            {5}                         <- fire starts here

Time 1:         {2, 7, 8}                      <- 5's neighbors: parent(2),
                                                   left(7), right(8)

Time 2:            {1, 4}                      <- 2's neighbors: parent(1),
                                                   left(4)  [2 & 5 visited]

Time 3:            {3}                         <- 1's neighbor: right(3)
                                                   [1 already visited]

All 8 nodes visited in 3 rounds  ->  answer = 3
```

Each "round" of the BFS = 1 minute, and the round only counts if at least
one new node was reached in it (this avoids an extra `+1` after the fire
has already finished spreading).

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

## Code

```java
/* Structure of binary tree node
class Node {
    int data;
    Node left;
    Node right;
    Node(int data) {
        this.data = data;
        left = right = null;
    }
}*/

class Solution {                                                     // class holding the solution method
    public int minTime(Node root, int target) {                      // method to find minimum burn time

        Map<Node, Node> parent = new HashMap<>();                    // map to store each node's parent

        Queue<Node> q = new LinkedList<>();                          // queue used for BFS traversal

        Node start = null;                                           // reference to the target node

        q.add(root);                                                 // add root to begin first BFS

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {                                       // loop until all nodes are visited

            Node cur = q.poll();                                     // remove front node from queue

            if (cur.data == target)                                  // check if current node is the target
                start = cur;                                         // save reference to target node

            if (cur.left != null) {                                  // if left child exists
                parent.put(cur.left, cur);                           // record cur as parent of left child
                q.add(cur.left);                                     // enqueue left child
            }

            if (cur.right != null) {                                 // if right child exists
                parent.put(cur.right, cur);                          // record cur as parent of right child
                q.add(cur.right);                                    // enqueue right child
            }
        }

        q.add(start);                                                // start second BFS from target node

        Set<Node> visited = new HashSet<>();                         // set to track visited nodes

        visited.add(start);                                          // mark target node as visited

        int time = 0;                                                // counts minutes taken to burn tree

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {                                       // loop until fire stops spreading

            int n = q.size();                                        // number of nodes at current level

            boolean flag = false;                                    // tracks if progress was made this round

            while (n-- > 0) {                                        // process all nodes in current level

                Node cur = q.poll();                                 // remove front node from queue

                if (cur.left != null && visited.add(cur.left)) {     // try spreading to left child
                    q.add(cur.left);                                 // enqueue newly reached left child
                    flag = true;                                     // mark progress made
                }

                if (cur.right != null && visited.add(cur.right)) {   // try spreading to right child
                    q.add(cur.right);                                // enqueue newly reached right child
                    flag = true;                                     // mark progress made
                }

                Node p = parent.get(cur);                            // fetch parent of current node
                if (p != null && visited.add(p)) {                   // try spreading to parent
                    q.add(p);                                        // enqueue newly reached parent
                    flag = true;                                     // mark progress made
                }
            }

            if (flag)                                                // only count round if progress made
                time++;                                              // increment time by one minute
        }

        return time;                                                 // return total burn time
    }
}
```

////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

## Complexity

- Time: `O(N)` — every node is visited a constant number of times.
- Space: `O(N)` — for the `parent` map, `visited` set, and the queue.
